1
00:00:00,800 --> 00:00:03,320
Hi! In this video I'll run DenseSym,

2
00:00:03,170 --> 00:00:08,126
our compressed compiler symbol table, and show you how the website works.

3
00:00:08,326 --> 00:00:13,674
Every compiler keeps a symbol table: for each identifier it records the type,

4
00:00:13,524 --> 00:00:17,116
the scope, the storage class and the memory offset.

5
00:00:17,316 --> 00:00:20,351
The textbook version is a chained hash table,

6
00:00:20,201 --> 00:00:26,250
which pays for 8-byte pointers and a separate heap allocation for every node and every name.

7
00:00:26,450 --> 00:00:34,212
DenseSym stores exactly the same information with three techniques: a Patricia trie that shares common prefixes,

8
00:00:34,062 --> 00:00:40,736
flat arenas addressed by 16-bit indices, and all the attributes bit-packed into one 32-bit word.

9
00:00:42,586 --> 00:00:44,200
This is the workbench.

10
00:00:44,400 --> 00:00:48,115
On the left is a C source editor with ready-made presets,

11
00:00:47,965 --> 00:00:51,054
and on the right are three views of the result.

12
00:00:51,254 --> 00:00:57,532
Right now the site is in offline mode: it shows results that our C engine computed in advance,

13
00:00:57,382 --> 00:01:00,140
so the page works even without a server.

14
00:01:00,340 --> 00:01:06,590
The first preset is an embedded sensor module with 30 global identifiers,

15
00:01:06,440 --> 00:01:09,682
like counter, temp_max and sensor_id.

16
00:01:11,532 --> 00:01:14,394
The Metrics tab answers the main question.

17
00:01:14,594 --> 00:01:23,552
The textbook hash table needs 2,725 bytes for these 30 names, and DenseSym needs 669.

18
00:01:23,752 --> 00:01:28,342
That's 75% less memory, or about 4.1 times denser.

19
00:01:28,542 --> 00:01:33,684
Below that, the ablation benchmark switches each technique on and off.

20
00:01:33,884 --> 00:01:40,138
Bit-packing alone saves under 9%, and the trie on its own actually makes the table bigger,

21
00:01:39,988 --> 00:01:44,954
because every trie node is a separate heap object with 8-byte pointers.

22
00:01:45,154 --> 00:01:50,541
Arena allocation with bit-packing delivers almost the whole saving,

23
00:01:50,391 --> 00:01:54,136
and all three techniques together reach 75.4%.

24
00:01:54,636 --> 00:01:57,906
At the bottom is peak memory with scope release.

25
00:01:58,106 --> 00:02:03,648
If the compiler frees each block's symbols when the block closes,

26
00:02:03,498 --> 00:02:08,792
the largest table it ever needs drops from 2,432 bytes to 601.

27
00:02:10,642 --> 00:02:15,208
The Symbol Table tab lists every declaration the engine extracted.

28
00:02:15,408 --> 00:02:20,766
Each row has the type, the scope depth, the storage class and the memory offset.

29
00:02:20,966 --> 00:02:25,808
The last column is the 32-bit word DenseSym actually stores:

30
00:02:25,658 --> 00:02:30,812
4 bits of type, 8 of scope, 4 of storage class and 16 of offset.

31
00:02:31,012 --> 00:02:35,026
I can also search it, so let me look for everything named temp.

32
00:02:36,876 --> 00:02:41,130
The Radix Trie Visualizer shows how the names are actually stored.

33
00:02:41,330 --> 00:02:46,832
Every box is a 10-byte node in the arena, labelled with its arena index.

34
00:02:47,032 --> 00:02:53,117
Shared prefixes are stored only once: counter and config share "co",

35
00:02:52,967 --> 00:02:56,782
which then branches into "unt" and "nfig".

36
00:02:56,982 --> 00:03:02,796
41 nodes and 139 bytes of characters hold all 30 names.

37
00:03:04,646 --> 00:03:08,660
Different code behaves differently, so let's try another preset.

38
00:03:09,860 --> 00:03:16,058
In this UART driver, every function reuses the same local names, like i and len.

39
00:03:16,258 --> 00:03:25,530
DenseSym saves 81.65% here: a name declared again in another function costs only a packed word and a small shadow record,

40
00:03:25,380 --> 00:03:28,696
not a new node and a new copy of the name.

41
00:03:29,396 --> 00:03:34,298
And here is the worst case: identifiers that share no prefixes at all.

42
00:03:34,498 --> 00:03:37,330
The trie has almost nothing to share,

43
00:03:37,180 --> 00:03:41,680
but the arena and bit-packing still cut the memory by 77.9%.

44
00:03:43,530 --> 00:03:47,760
Now the most important part: analyzing code that I write myself.

45
00:03:47,960 --> 00:03:52,815
I'll switch to Live Backend. The badge now says the engine is online,

46
00:03:52,665 --> 00:03:59,294
so every request goes to our FastAPI service, which runs the C analyzer as a sandboxed process.

47
00:03:59,794 --> 00:04:02,945
I'll clear the editor and type a small program:

48
00:04:02,795 --> 00:04:07,288
two global sensor variables, and a function with one local variable.

49
00:04:09,488 --> 00:04:14,554
When I press Analyze, the engine tokenizes the code, tracks every scope,

50
00:04:14,404 --> 00:04:20,630
builds all eight structures, and verifies every lookup before it reports a single number.

51
00:04:20,830 --> 00:04:27,428
For these four declarations, the textbook table needs 815 bytes,

52
00:04:27,278 --> 00:04:30,652
and DenseSym only 121: 85% less.

53
00:04:30,852 --> 00:04:34,638
In the symbol table, local_val sits at scope depth 1,

54
00:04:34,488 --> 00:04:37,794
inside the function, with its own packed word.

55
00:04:37,994 --> 00:04:43,426
The service is hardened too: the code is only parsed and never run,

56
00:04:43,276 --> 00:04:47,840
input is capped at 64 KB, and requests are rate limited.

57
00:04:49,690 --> 00:04:51,688
Next, the Hardware tab.

58
00:04:51,888 --> 00:04:58,125
We ran all eight structures on a real Arduino Uno, which has only 2 KB of RAM,

59
00:04:57,975 --> 00:05:03,198
and everything here was measured on the board: 468 runs in total.

60
00:05:03,398 --> 00:05:11,540
For the 30-name preset, the textbook table takes 901 bytes on the board, and DenseSym 669.

61
00:05:11,740 --> 00:05:17,092
With 50 globals, the textbook table runs out of memory at name 49,

62
00:05:16,942 --> 00:05:19,378
while DenseSym stores all 50.

63
00:05:19,578 --> 00:05:24,164
And when an Uno is plugged in, the page connects to it over Web Serial,

64
00:05:24,014 --> 00:05:27,912
so any program in the editor can be built live on the board.

65
00:05:29,762 --> 00:05:33,999
The site also hosts a second tool: the syntax table suite,

66
00:05:33,849 --> 00:05:37,664
which compresses the parse tables an LR parser uses.

67
00:05:37,864 --> 00:05:44,578
For the ANSI C grammar, row displacement shrinks the table from 51,544 bytes to 12,844,

68
00:05:44,428 --> 00:05:50,086
and the suite also measures lookup speed, build time and cache behaviour.

69
00:05:51,936 --> 00:05:55,038
Everything you've seen is open source on GitHub.

70
00:05:55,238 --> 00:06:01,454
The repository has setup, test and run scripts, the datasets with their download links,

71
00:06:01,304 --> 00:06:07,868
every result file, and the manuscript, both as a PDF and as an Overleaf-ready LaTeX project.

72
00:06:08,068 --> 00:06:12,732
A workflow rebuilds the whole project on a clean machine at every push,

73
00:06:12,582 --> 00:06:15,466
and checks that every result still matches.

74
00:06:16,066 --> 00:06:18,088
Thanks for watching!
